    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a clean, additive parallel of the existing sibling endpoints' guard steps, and a maintainer of this codebase would find it entirely natural.\n\n- **Logic in the right unit.** The daily-limit check lives in its own `EnsureDailyRegistrationLimit` function class, exactly like `EnsureCityCapacity`, `EnsureUniqueOwnerHousehold`, and `EnsureUniqueOwnerTelephone`. It is wired as a distinct `ensureDailyRegistrationLimit` step under `govern: [ transaction ]`, placed among the other `ensure*` guards before `build` \u2014 the correct position for a pre-save rejection.\n- **Exception/handler/YAML trio matches convention.** `DailyRegistrationLimitException` mirrors `CityAtCapacityException` (checked, `date`/`count` fields, message in the constructor, javadoc pointing at the handler). `DailyRegistrationLimitExceptionHandler` mirrors `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler` precisely \u2014 `ProblemDetails.build(...)` plus `setProperty` for structured detail, then a `ResponseEntity.status(...)`. The escalation YAML follows the `<fqcn>.yml \u2192 handle:` shape used by the others.\n- **No duplication or catch-all.** Nothing is bolted onto an existing class; each concern (count, exception, handler, wiring) sits where the codebase already puts its equivalent.\n- **Idiomatic counting.** Iterating `ownerRepository.findAll()` and comparing per-record is the established idiom here (`EnsureCityCapacity` and `EnsureUniqueOwnerHousehold` do the same), and it correctly declares only the dependency it needs (`OwnerRepository`, no `@Val` request since it counts by date only).",
